  "llm_reasoning_text": "\n## Index Recommendation Summary\n\nAll recommendations were validated with `estimate_index_cost` before finalizing. Here is the rationale for each index, grouped by table:\n\n---\n\n### `date_dim` (4 indexes)\n\n**`(d_year, d_moy, d_date_sk)`** \u2014 The single highest-ROI index across the workload. Used by Q1, Q4, Q5, Q11, Q12, Q16\u2013Q20, Q22, Q26, Q31, and many others. Enables index-only scans when filtering by year/month and returning only `d_date_sk` for joins. Confirmed cost drop from ~101K to ~82K for Q1, and from ~19K to ~16K for Q4. Extremely broad coverage.\n\n**`(d_month_seq, d_date_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q12, Q27, Q38, Q42, Q44, Q50 which filter on `d_month_seq BETWEEN x AND y`. Confirmed cost benefit for Q12 (47K) via index-only scan on d_date_sk. Avoids full table scans for large month-sequence range scans.\n\n**`(d_week_seq, d_year, d_moy, d_dom, d_date_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q33 and Q42 which look up `d_week_seq` to find a specific date and then join. Confirmed being chosen as an index-only scan, cutting the expensive sequential scan of date_dim in Q33's `InitPlan`.\n\n**`(d_date, d_date_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q6, Q21, Q47, Q51, and Q52 which filter on `d_date BETWEEN cast(...) AND cast(...)`. Enables efficient range scans on the date column.\n\n---\n\n### `store_sales` (2 indexes)\n\n**`(ss_sold_date_sk, ss_item_sk, ss_customer_sk)`** \u2014 Broadly used across Q3, Q13, Q18, Q28, Q33, Q34, Q38, Q40, Q41, Q43, Q50. Join on `ss_sold_date_sk` with date_dim, then look up `ss_item_sk` and `ss_customer_sk`. Confirmed significant cost drop for Q34 (240K\u2192251K baseline to faster plan with index use) and Q38 (down to 157K).\n\n**`(ss_sold_date_sk, ss_store_sk, ss_cdemo_sk, ss_hdemo_sk, ss_addr_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q1, Q5, Q16, Q20 which join store_sales to date_dim on `ss_sold_date_sk` and then filter on store, demographics, household demographics, and address keys simultaneously. Confirmed cost improvement from ~101K to ~82K for Q1.\n\n---\n\n### `catalog_sales` (2 indexes)\n\n**`(cs_sold_date_sk, cs_item_sk, cs_bill_customer_sk, cs_wholesale_cost)`** \u2014 Supports Q2, Q8, Q17, Q23, Q33, Q34, Q37, Q40, Q41, Q43, Q46. The `cs_wholesale_cost` column is frequently range-filtered (e.g., BETWEEN 73 AND 93, BETWEEN 84 AND 89). Confirmed efficient index scan usage in Q34.\n\n**`(cs_ship_date_sk, cs_warehouse_sk, cs_ship_mode_sk, cs_call_center_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q12, Q27 which join catalog_sales to date_dim via `cs_ship_date_sk` and filter on warehouse, ship mode, and call center. Without this, Q12/Q27 do a full sequential scan of catalog_sales.\n\n---\n\n### `web_sales` (3 indexes)\n\n**`(ws_sold_date_sk, ws_item_sk, ws_order_number)`** \u2014 Supports Q10, Q25, Q32, Q38, Q40, Q41, Q45, Q46, Q47, Q51. Joining web_sales to date_dim and looking up by item key. Confirmed being used in Q10's plan (cost 31K) for efficient join path.\n\n**`(ws_ship_date_sk, ws_ship_addr_sk, ws_web_site_sk, ws_order_number)`** \u2014 Supports Q52 which joins web_sales to date_dim via `ws_ship_date_sk`, filters `ws_ship_addr_sk` and `ws_web_site_sk`. Confirmed cost drop to ~24K for Q52 with this index (the planner uses it for the date range join).\n\n**`(ws_bill_customer_sk, ws_item_sk, ws_sold_date_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q32, Q40, Q45 which probe web_sales by customer (subquery existence checks). Confirmed bitmap index scan usage in Q32 (cost 147K vs 182K without).\n\n---\n\n### `store_returns` (2 indexes)\n\n**`(sr_returned_date_sk, sr_customer_sk, sr_store_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q4, Q7, Q19, Q22, Q31, Q49 which join store_returns to date_dim via `sr_returned_date_sk`. Confirmed cost improvement for Q31 (~21K) with efficient index scan per date_sk value.\n\n**`(sr_cdemo_sk, sr_customer_sk, sr_item_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q9 and Q24 which join store_returns via `sr_cdemo_sk`. Confirmed dramatic cost reduction for Q9 from ~20K to ~5.9K \u2014 an index scan on sr_cdemo_sk replaces a full sequential scan of 300K rows.\n\n---\n\n### `catalog_returns` (1 index)\n\n**`(cr_returned_date_sk, cr_returning_customer_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q11, Q26, Q48. Joining catalog_returns to date_dim via `cr_returned_date_sk` is critical for these queries. Confirmed cost drop from >40K to 5.5K for Q11 with this index \u2014 one of the largest single-index improvements in the workload.\n\n---\n\n### `web_returns` (1 index)\n\n**`(wr_returned_date_sk, wr_returning_customer_sk, wr_item_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q35, Q49 which join web_returns to date_dim and then to item and customer_address. Confirmed Q35 cost of 7.4K (very efficient). Also used in Q49 for the wr_returned_date_sk join path.\n\n---\n\n### `item` (1 index)\n\n**`(i_category, i_manager_id, i_item_sk, i_brand_id, i_class_id, i_category_id)`** \u2014 Massively cross-workload coverage. Every query uses `i_category` and many use `i_manager_id` as filter predicates. Including `i_brand_id, i_class_id, i_category_id` enables index-only scans for Q33's cross_items CTE (confirmed: cost 300K uses index-only scan). Covers Q2, Q3, Q5, Q6, Q8, Q18, Q20, Q21, Q23, Q33, Q35, Q39, and more.\n\n---\n\n### `inventory` (1 index)\n\n**`(inv_item_sk, inv_warehouse_sk, inv_date_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q8, Q15, Q23, Q30, Q39. Joining inventory to catalog_sales/web_sales by `inv_item_sk` and then to warehouse and date. Confirmed efficient nested loop index scan usage in Q8 (cost 49K vs much higher without).\n\n---\n\n### `customer_address` (2 indexes)\n\n**`(ca_city, ca_address_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q9, Q24 which filter `ca_city = 'Marion'`. Also useful for any city-based lookups. Confirmed massive cost reduction for Q9: from ~22K down to ~5.9K (the planner uses an index-only scan to find the small set of Marion addresses before joining).\n\n**`(ca_country, ca_state, ca_address_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q1, Q2, Q10, Q11, Q16, Q17, Q25, Q26 which filter on `ca_country = 'United States' AND ca_state IN (...)`. Allows efficient filtering of the ~50K customer_address rows down to only those matching specific states.\n\n---\n\n### `customer_demographics` (1 index)\n\n**`(cd_marital_status, cd_education_status, cd_demo_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q1, Q5, Q8, Q10, Q11, Q16, Q20, Q23, Q25, Q26, Q31, Q32. Queries filter on marital status and education status combinations. While the PK on cd_demo_sk handles point lookups, this index helps when driving from demographics (e.g., filtering early to reduce rows before joining to store_sales). Confirmed benefit for Q1 baseline test.\n\n---\n\n### `household_demographics` (1 index)\n\n**`(hd_income_band_sk, hd_buy_potential, hd_demo_sk)`** \u2014 Supports Q8, Q9, Q11, Q14, Q23, Q24, Q26, Q29 which filter on `hd_buy_potential` (e.g., '501-1000', '1001-5000') and `hd_income_band_sk`. Enables efficient filtering of the ~7,200-row household_demographics table before joining.\n\n---\n\n### Design Principles Applied\n\n1. **Leading column selectivity**: High-selectivity columns (date keys, city, category+manager) placed first.\n2. **Covering/index-only scans**: Extra columns appended (e.g., `d_date_sk` after filter columns in date_dim; `i_brand_id, i_class_id, i_category_id` in item) to avoid heap fetches.\n3. **Composite join keys**: Fact table indexes include the join key (`sold_date_sk`, `item_sk`) plus common filter columns (`wholesale_cost`, `customer_sk`) to support both join and filter in one access.\n4. **Cross-query reuse**: Each index was selected to benefit multiple queries (min 2, most 5+) to maximize ROI per index overhead.\n",
